**Shipped: 0.11.408** (`50D0591F24309A58B91BFE4`) — the wire format for the durable recovery descriptor that sess63's GPT ruling made the prerequisite for everything else on `D-FOREIGN-SLICE-INTENTS-ABANDONED`. Type-level only; nothing reads or writes it yet, so runtime behavior is byte-identical to 0.11.407. The version rev exists so the srcversion drift isn't mistaken for an untracked build.

**The placement result matters more than the struct.** The HB record is exactly 512 B with zero reserved space, so I expected to have to shrink the evict ring 25→20 entries to make room. That turned out to be unnecessary: the descriptor only ever exists in a record whose `flags == RECOVERY_GUARD`, and the evict ring is produced only into a node's own ACTIVE record and consumed only from a peer's ACTIVE record — the ring consumer at `dlm/disklock.c:607` sits downstream of the `flags != ACTIVE` bail at `:557`. So a guard record's 416 ring bytes are dead space in every existing build. The descriptor is a union overlaying them: no region growth, no mkfs change, no ring-depth regression, and each member's own magic keeps the two interpretations from ever misreading each other.

**Found and fixed a latent build-guard defect:** `MXFS_BUILD_CHECK_HB()` is referenced by nothing in the tree, so the kernel half of the on-disk layout guard has never validated anything — the only live checks were the user-mode `_Static_assert`s and the pair at `disklock.c:65`. My new layout asserts are outside the `#ifdef` so they compile in both. Verified by a deliberate failure first: I'd written `offsetof(…, mepoch) == 392` (arithmetic from the discarded ring-shrink plan; the real value is 456) and the user-mode branch caught it. Both builds are clean now.

The five design rules GPT ruled binding — durable-CAS-before-any-purge, victim identity never overwritten, the split broadcast predicate, the victim-manifest freeze, and stale-descriptor-is-a-recovery-lease-never-a-member-slot — are encoded in the header commentary so the `.c` implementation can't drift from them.

Next is the `.c` side: begin/advance/refresh/read/takeover, the split predicate at `disklock.c:493-522`, the purge freeze, and one non-obvious hole I traced — `find_node_slot`'s disk-scan arm matches ACTIVE|WITHDRAWN only, so a peer that never saw the death would resolve slot=-1 and fall into the "owns no slice → purge immediately" arm at `v5_mount.c:1413`. The in-memory `slot_node_id[]` fast path masks that for peers that did see the death, which is why it's easy to miss. All of it is written down in ccmemory with line numbers.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 14 open defects, 7 critical, and no rig cycle since 0.11.401.

